WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:03.820
Nowadays, a new kind of sport is attracting a new breed of players.

2
00:00:03.821 --> 00:00:10.598
These athletes don’t belong to a team, they don’t wear a uniform, and they often compete against no one but themselves.

3
00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:14.840
The sports they play are dangerous, crazy, and even death-defying.

4
00:00:14.841 --> 00:00:17.641
Welcome to the world of extreme sports.

5
00:00:17.642 --> 00:00:22.895
Extreme sports have a relatively short history and became popular only in the 1980s.

6
00:00:22.896 --> 00:00:31.666
They include snowboarding, skateboarding, bungee jumping, BASE jumping, indoor climbing, bouldering, and BMX, as well as many more.

7
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:39.022
These sports are called "extreme" because they often combine speed, height, danger, and difficult stunts.

8
00:00:39.023 --> 00:00:43.935
The rise of extreme sports signals a change in modern attitudes toward sport.

9
00:00:43.936 --> 00:00:51.434
The individual is emphasized rather than the team, and players are bound not by rules, but only by the limits of safety.

10
00:00:51.435 --> 00:00:58.156
Traditional Western sports, like soccer or hockey, are played in teams with clear sets of rules and regular matches.

11
00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:03.112
Extreme sports, on the other hand, are all about individual effort and accomplishment.

12
00:01:03.113 --> 00:01:08.168
The athletes push themselves to conquer their fears and challenge the limits of skill and daring.

13
00:01:08.169 --> 00:01:13.902
Even though extreme sports involve pushing against limits, safety is always a priority.

14
00:01:13.903 --> 00:01:21.481
Rugby, a type of football, may in fact be more dangerous than many extreme sports because most rugby players do not wear protective clothing.

15
00:01:21.482 --> 00:01:27.282
In comparison, participants in extreme sports wear pads, helmets, and other protective gear.

16
00:01:27.283 --> 00:01:35.997
Most participants in extreme sports are young men, but women are participating more and more and getting recognized for their skill and achievements.

17
00:01:35.998 --> 00:01:38.711
Snowboarding is a winter extreme sport.

18
00:01:38.712 --> 00:01:45.666
Snowboarders strap one boot to a wide board and, balancing on their snowboard, slide down snow-covered slopes.

19
00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:48.722
Snowboarding is similar to surfing and skateboarding.

20
00:01:48.723 --> 00:01:57.909
Snowboards were first made in 1977, but only recently have snowboarders been accepted on mountains that until now have been dedicated to skiing.

21
00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:06.338
Like skiers, snowboarders travel down mountain slopes, but a big part of the sport is also learning and perfecting jumps, stunts, and tricks.

22
00:02:06.339 --> 00:02:13.687
Snowboarding hills often include jump ramps and a "halfpipe," which is formed by two long, curved ramps that face each other.

23
00:02:13.688 --> 00:02:19.969
Boarders jump off the ramps and the top edges of the halfpipe, performing tricks while they fly through the air.

24
00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:27.279
At the 1998 Winter Games in Nagano, Japan, snowboarding was the first extreme sport to become part of the Olympics.

25
00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.089
Competitors jumped into the air as they snowboarded down the halfpipe.

26
00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:37.983
They were judged on how high they flew, how many spins and stunts they did, and their overall performance.

27
00:02:37.984 --> 00:02:41.229
Skateboarding is one of the oldest extreme sports.

28
00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:47.689
It was originally called "sidewalk surfing," and skateboards were first commercially produced in the 1960s.

29
00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:57.008
The first skateboards were very narrow, flimsy, and unstable─if you ran over a pebble, the skateboard would stop!─and they weren’t too popular.

30
00:02:57.009 --> 00:03:03.515
It wasn’t until the 1970s that skateboard design evolved and skateboards became wider and easier to ride.

31
00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:09.226
Skateboarding took off among teenagers and young people, and tricks like the "ollie" were invented.

32
00:03:09.227 --> 00:03:14.733
Skateboarders perform an ollie by kicking one end of their board down just as they jump into the air.

33
00:03:14.734 --> 00:03:21.487
The board bounces into the air with the rider, allowing the rider to jump over objects as he or she rolls along.

34
00:03:21.488 --> 00:03:27.897
Nowadays, "skaters" form a unique subculture with distinctive clothes, music, and hairstyles.

35
00:03:27.898 --> 00:03:34.570
In the extreme sport of bungee jumping, people jump off very high places with a piece of elastic cord tied to their bodies.

36
00:03:34.571 --> 00:03:39.503
Bungee jumpers fall almost to the ground, stretching the cord until it snaps back.

37
00:03:39.504 --> 00:03:41.916
Then they fly back up through the air.

38
00:03:41.917 --> 00:03:51.334
In the 1950s, filmmaker Richard Attenborough filmed native tribesmen in Vanuatu jumping from tall wooden platforms with vines tied to their ankles. This

39
00:03:51.335 --> 00:03:59.545
footage inspired an Englishman, Chris Baker, to copy the stunt with elastic, and in 1979 bungee jumping was born. Since

40
00:03:59.546 --> 00:04:07.196
the mid 1980s, bungee jumpers have leapt off bridges, cliffs, cranes, and tall buildings, including the Eiffel Tower. In

41
00:04:07.197 --> 00:04:11.902
the movie Golden Eye , James Bond bungee jumps off a tall dam.

42
00:04:11.903 --> 00:04:21.978
Over a million bungee jumps have been done worldwide, but falling dizzying heights attached only to an elastic cord will never be a sport for the faint-hearted!

43
00:04:21.979 --> 00:04:31.940
BASE jumping has perhaps received the most media attention of all extreme sports because of its daring nature and the not infrequent deaths resulting from it.

44
00:04:31.941 --> 00:04:42.022
BASE jumping is very similar to skydiving, except that BASE jumpers jump off a fixed object like a bridge or a tall building instead of out of an airplane.

45
00:04:42.023 --> 00:04:46.964
Their parachutes must open quickly and they must land in a very small area.

46
00:04:46.965 --> 00:04:54.910
The vast majority of BASE jumpers are experienced skydivers, but the sport still reports many injuries and deaths every year.

47
00:04:54.911 --> 00:05:04.546
One difficulty BASE jumpers face is finding places to jump off, because many building owners are reluctant to give permission for people to parachute off their buildings.

48
00:05:04.547 --> 00:05:12.660
At only one place in the world─the Perrine Memorial Bridge in Idaho─can BASE jumpers jump legally every day of the year.

49
00:05:12.661 --> 00:05:20.504
On Bridge Day, held once a year at this bridge, over eight hundred BASE jumps are made in just over six hours!

50
00:05:20.505 --> 00:05:26.124
Rock climbing has become such a popular extreme sport that now you can do it almost anywhere.

51
00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:35.612
You can climb outdoor rock faces, mountains, and cliffs, but you can also climb in the city on climbing walls in indoor climbing gyms!

52
00:05:35.613 --> 00:05:46.694
A climbing wall is a fake cliff specially designed for indoor climbing, with grips for climber’s hands and feet and special protective mats on the floor at the base of the wall.

53
00:05:46.695 --> 00:05:52.995
Bouldering is a kind of rock climbing in which the athletes climb large boulders or rock faces without a rope.

54
00:05:52.996 --> 00:06:03.204
In bouldering, figuring out the best climbing route, problem-solving, and technical skills are more important than strength and endurance because the climb is often short.

55
00:06:03.205 --> 00:06:07.956
But it’s usually interesting─just like the boulders themselves!

56
00:06:07.957 --> 00:06:17.473
And, finally, there’s the extreme sport of BMX, which involves racing and performing stunts on a kind of bicycle called "BMX."

57
00:06:17.474 --> 00:06:24.166
BMX is thought to have started in the 1970s when riders modified their bicycles to make them more exciting.

58
00:06:24.167 --> 00:06:34.913
The modified bikes had tougher tires and stronger frames, which allowed riders to race on dirt tracks, jump in the air, and even balance and spin on their bikes.

59
00:06:34.914 --> 00:06:45.208
BMX racing is popular among younger children and teenagers because all they need to compete is a bike, some knee and arm pads, and a good bike helmet.

60
00:06:45.209 --> 00:06:54.417
BMX freestyle, in which the riders perform tricks and stunts, will be an official sport at the 2008 Olympics in Beijing.

61
00:06:54.418 --> 00:07:00.177
As more and more people become extreme sports enthusiasts, the way we think of sport is changing.

62
00:07:00.178 --> 00:07:07.983
Players participate in order to test themselves and challenge their physical abilities or to experience a new, exciting thrill.

63
00:07:07.984 --> 00:07:21.101
Some critics argue that corporate marketing has ruined the spirit of extreme sports by turning it into corporate slogans, such as "live life to the max" (Pepsi) and "just do it" (Nike).

64
00:07:21.102 --> 00:07:36.297
But while extreme sports may be the hot new trend in advertising, the values of extreme sports─practice, perseverance, risk, safety, and fun─make them just as challenging and thrilling as ever!

